<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
        The first widening of the custody action list, and it arrives with the code that
        emits the new values — which is the rule 0006 set when it defined only CREATED: an
        action list is read as exhaustive, so a value nothing writes turns "never recorded"
        into a false claim of "never happened". IntegrityCheckService now exists and
        re-verifies a preserved artifact against the digest recorded for it, so the two
        outcomes of that check are now something the ledger can honestly hold.

        Two actions rather than one with a boolean in the detail, because the detail is
        free text hashed into the digest and not something a query can filter on. "Every
        artifact that has ever failed re-verification" is the question this system exists
        to answer, and it should be one WHERE clause on an indexed column, not a full-text
        search over prose.

        How the artifact hash pair reads on these entries, stated here because it departs
        from the "before and after the action" reading 0006 documents for CREATED: a
        verification does not change the bytes, so on HASH_VERIFIED and HASH_MISMATCH the
        pair is the digest *expected* (evidence_item.current_hash at the time of the check)
        and the digest *observed* (recomputed from the object read back out of storage).
        On HASH_VERIFIED the two are equal; on HASH_MISMATCH they are not, and the entry is
        the durable record of exactly what was found — which is why no separate
        integrity_check table is created here. README open question 10 argues that two
        records of the same event drift and that one ordered ledger is easier to defend than
        two that must be reconciled; the same argument applies to re-verification results,
        and it applies more strongly, because a mismatch is the single most consequential
        finding this system can make and it should sit in the chain that cannot be edited
        rather than in a table that can.

        What a HASH_MISMATCH entry does NOT do is change evidence_item.current_hash. That
        column holds the digest the artifact is supposed to have; a mismatch means the
        stored object no longer produces it, and overwriting the expectation with the
        observation would erase the very discrepancy being recorded. The item's status
        moves to COMPROMISED instead, in the same transaction as the entry.

        No backfill and no data migration: widening a CHECK touches no rows, and every
        existing entry is CREATED, which remains valid.
    -->

    <changeSet id="0007-1" author="dfraas-team">
        <comment>Widen ck_custody_event_action to admit the two outcomes of an integrity re-verification</comment>
        <!--
            DROP then ADD rather than ALTER, because Postgres cannot alter a CHECK expression
            in place. Postgres validates the new constraint against every existing row when
            it is added, which is the point: this is a superset of the old list, so it must
            pass, and if it did not the migration should fail rather than leave a row the
            constraint disagrees with.

            The list must stay in step with the CustodyAction enum.
        -->
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE custody_event DROP CONSTRAINT ck_custody_event_action;
        </sql>
        <sql>
            ALTER TABLE custody_event
                ADD CONSTRAINT ck_custody_event_action
                CHECK (action IN ('CREATED', 'HASH_VERIFIED', 'HASH_MISMATCH'));
        </sql>
        <!--
            The rollback restores the 0006 list, and it will refuse to run if any
            HASH_VERIFIED or HASH_MISMATCH entry exists — which is correct. Those rows are
            custody entries, and a rollback that deleted them to make the narrower
            constraint fit would be the one thing this table exists to prevent. Rolling
            this changeset back on a database that has run an integrity check is a decision
            to be made with the ledger in front of you, not by a migration tool.
        -->
        <rollback>
            <sql>ALTER TABLE custody_event DROP CONSTRAINT ck_custody_event_action;</sql>
            <sql>
                ALTER TABLE custody_event
                    ADD CONSTRAINT ck_custody_event_action
                    CHECK (action IN ('CREATED'));
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
